00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.275
So let's get started.

00:01:51.275 --> 00:01:52.650
You know, our
first load balancer

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.410
was our network load balancer.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.220
Three years ago, we
published a paper on Maglev,

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:02.520
and it's our actually in-house
developed software load

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.450
balancer.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.520
It runs on standard
Google hardware,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.090
but they optimized the code
for throughput and latency.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.910
And this has been in
production since 2008,

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.820
and it literally load
balances all traffic coming

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.470
into Google's data centers.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:21.510
It also distributes traffic to
our front end engines located

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.420
in our points of
presence, and now we

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.440
make them available to
you as our network load

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.470
balancer for Google Cloud.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.200
So a network load balancer
essentially load balances

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.780
layer 4 TCP or UDP traffic via
hashing to regional back end

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.450
instances.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.890
The Maglevs tunnel
the packet directly

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:47.680
to the back end instance, so
the source IP is preserved.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.510
The back end instances return
the packet to the client

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:52.070
directly.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.820
It's direct server
written, so it does not

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.880
go through the
Maglevs In this slide,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.370
you see three regional
load balancers--

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.730
test, my app, and travel.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.640
What's interesting is that
these regional load balances--

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:09.570
the virtual IP addresses-- they
come from a regional block,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.030
but when you're using
the premium tier,

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.200
we advertise them globally.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.790
So if Shen in Singapore
wanted to access the load

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.134
balancer located in
the US West, myapp.com,

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:25.860
their traffic would ingress
the port in Singapore,

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.300
go through our Google
global network,

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.170
and be load balanced at US West.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.300
So even though it's a
regional load balancer,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:38.698
you have global access with
network load balancing.

00:03:38.698 --> 00:03:39.740
So what's under the hood?

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.600
Remember I was
talking about Maglevs?

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.370
So compare and contrast.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.490
When you have traditional
load balancers,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:48.920
they're typically deployed
in an active standby fashion,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.530
and they typically need
to be pre-warmed to deal

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.640
with spikes in traffic.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.880
That's the one on the left.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.310
And on the right, you
have your Maglevs.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:03:59.840
And these Maglevs,
they're actually

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.530
deployed in Active/Active
scale-out fashion.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.840
The way it works is
that the Maglevs--

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.320
they advertise the virtual
IP address of your load

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.510
balancers-- all of them
to the peering router.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:16.100
The peering router then
equal-cost multi path

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.533
distributes the
flows to the Maglevs.

00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:21.200
What do you want out
of a load balancer?

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.590
You want to evenly
distribute your workloads,

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.400
and you want a stable
connection from your client

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.560
to your back end instance.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.170
And the Maglevs do this
primarily in two ways.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:38.910
One, it does connection tracking
of existing connections.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:43.460
So if other back end
instances come up or go down,

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.540
your current connection keeps
tracked and stays stable.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.630
The second, it does something
called consistent hashing,

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.270
which means that regardless of
the set of Maglevs changing,

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.800
the back end is always
selected consistently.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.530
So that's essentially
Maglev under the hood.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:08.220
So just to recap what does a
network load balancer get you?

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.410
It's a regional load balancer.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.790
It's a layer 4 load balancer.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.200
You don't get layer 7.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.720
You can do 2, 3,
or 5-tuple hashing,

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.770
and it's very performant.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:20.600
You get almost a million
queries per second.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.330
But what does it not provide?

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:26.920
It does not provide you global
IPv4 and IPv6 load balancing.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:30.010
You cannot route on the
basis of layer 7 headers,

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:34.530
and you cannot do SSL proxy.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.610
So Google needed to come up
with a global load balancer.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.840
Now just to recap, think of
traditional load balancers

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.590
and public clouds.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:46.930
They are regional.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.930
They have a regional VIP
address and regional set

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.650
of back end instances.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.030
So let's say you have a
service that are distributed

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.530
in three different regions.

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.900
You have three different VIPs.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.920
Now if you wanted to globally
load balance across those three

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.920
regions, you need
a DNS load balancer

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.860
that would map the client
request to one of these VIPs,

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.140
but there are several
challenges with this approach.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:19.350
Imagine an instance in one
of the regions going away.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.600
The load balancing and
the DNS infrastructure

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.000
has to know of that change.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.840
Let's say your client
caches that IP address,

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.540
and that could result in
a suboptimal selection.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.600
Last, your capacity is siloed.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.600
Resources in one region cannot
be used in another region.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.350
Now that would simply
just not work for Google,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.720
so when we had to come
up with a load balancer

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.990
we did not use the
DNS-based approach.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.870
What we did was we went and
pushed the load balancing

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.430
to the edge of our
global network,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.770
and we came up with a single
global anycast virtual IP

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:01.940
address that front ends
worldwide capacity.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:06.140
If you run into availability
or capacity constraints,

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:10.640
you can do cross region
failover and fallback.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.770
It's very nicely tied to our
auto scaling infrastructure,

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.890
so when demand rises,
we can scale it up

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:17.720
to meet our requirements.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:20.900
And when demand ebbs,
we can scale it down.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:21.890
It's also really nice.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.390
It gives you the
single point where

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:27.380
you can apply global policies,
and it's very, very performant

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.750
because it's nearly no
single point instance that's

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:30.960
doing the load balancing.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.340
And you can support easily a
million queries per second.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.730
So how do our customers
typically deploy this, right?

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.560
You would typically
actually start out

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.470
deploying your services
in a single region,

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.410
but what you would do is you'd
configure a global forwarding

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:51.980
rule with a global anycast
virtual IP address.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:55.370
And you'd make your customer,
Maya in California, very happy.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.895
But let's say your service
grows in popularity,

00:07:58.895 --> 00:08:00.770
and now you have a
customer, Bob in New York,

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.110
that also wishes to
access the service.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:08.700
Similarly, you would deploy
back end instances in US East,

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:10.710
but you don't have to
reconfigure the VIP.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.870
You don't have to
modify any DNS entries.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.160
You're a global success.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.970
You have customers in Asia
that are now using back

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:23.980
end instances in Singapore.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.350
Unfortunately, it
[? suddenly ?] happened--

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:30.650
and you had instances
in Singapore go down.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.390
At the same time,
demand in US West

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:39.919
spiked, and you need to
address those events.

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:44.140
What will happen is, without
requiring any intervention,

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.690
your user requests
will seamlessly

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.160
be directed to the region with
the most available capacity.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.790
Now when the US West
scales up, the traffic

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:57.280
will again fall back to US West.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.040
Again, this is
happening all seamlessly

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:04.110
without any intervention.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:06.340
Now what is it under the hood?

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:09.270
This tool is also
software-based load balancing.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:12.640
The load balancing happens
at these things called Google

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:15.180
Front End engines, or GFEs.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.690
These GFEs are located at
the edges of our network.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:22.530
Now the edge itself is tiered.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.260
Some of the load balancing
happens in the tier one edge.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.480
Some of the load balancing
happens in the tier two.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:32.070
The tier one edge is where our
points of presence are located.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.610
The GFEs effectively
proxy the client traffic.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.130
And they load balanced
by working in concert

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.370
with each other--

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.660
working with our software
defined networking plane

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:48.270
and also our global
control and config plane.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:50.290
How do we distribute traffic?

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:53.530
Now, this slide is really dense,
but I have one key takeaway,

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:58.470
which is as long as you have
capacity in the region closest

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.890
to your users, we will
send the traffic there.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:06.910
But should you run into capacity
or availability constraints,

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.750
we will then direct
the traffic spill-over

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:12.480
to the next closest region.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.350
We call this our waterfall
model for load balancing.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.960
A region has multiple zones.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.360
We will populate each
zone proportional

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.570
to the capacity that you
have allocated to each zone,

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.720
and each zone we will
load balance evenly

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.900
among the instances in
a round robin fashion.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.460
Now it's quite a lot, and I
don't expect to remember this.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.490
Oz is going to show
this to you live,

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:38.610
but before we go to
the demo, I think

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:40.560
it's important
that we cover what

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.840
the data model for a
configuration looks like.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:47.880
In a load balancing, you have
something called the front end

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.810
and you have something
called the back end.

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:53.970
In the front end, you have
that global anycast VIP.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:57.540
That VIP is associated
with the forwarding rule.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:00.810
That forwarding rule
points to a target proxy.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:06.160
Now this target proxy could
be HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, or SSL.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:10.410
The target proxy is also the
location where have URL maps,

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:11.610
and URL map--

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.120
essentially, maps
in a client URL

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.500
based on host and path rules
to a specific back end service.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.710
Now this is where the
back end portion begins.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:25.050
A back end service is
made up of back ends.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:27.960
Back ends could be managed
instance groups or network

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:29.220
endpoint groups.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.500
The back end service
is also the location

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:34.320
where we associate
health configuration

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:35.940
and serving capacity.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.070
Oz is going to show all
of this to you right now,

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.130
so now let me just
hand it off to Oz

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.980
to show this to all to you live.

