00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.950
And everything else,
the default path

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:51.570
then is going to be sent
to a separate back end.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:57.250
And then last here is how do
we map those hosts and path

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.710
rules through the back ends?

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.940
So I have two back
ends here in this case.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:05.590
The first one, which is going to
handle all the default traffic,

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:08.040
I have configured as
the endpoint protocol

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:11.410
HTTPS, which again is
also our best practice.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:12.570
So make sure that--

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:14.590
we recommend having
TLS everywhere.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:17.130
So the first session,
just to recap,

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.830
between the client and
the VIP uses HTTPS.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:23.670
The second one for this back
end also relies on HTTPS.

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:26.100
I have also a named
port, which is a port

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.930
that you can define
for the communication

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:31.140
between the proxy
layer and the client.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.570
It doesn't have to be the
same one as the front end,

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:35.820
and we're going to see that
configuration as well when

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.880
we take a look at the
managed instance group.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.480
The next one is a
timeout, which is also

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.480
for the connection between
proxy and the back end,

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:48.330
so here you want to take
a look at your application

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.240
requirements.

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.220
So if you have any requirements
of using long-lived sessions,

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:56.540
you may want to relax
the timer a little bit

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:01.930
in order to not kill your
sessions before you want.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.570
Cloud CDN is also here.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:06.990
You can enable cloud CDN
on the back end level,

00:15:06.990 --> 00:15:09.630
and it's a very
simple configuration.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.850
It's a checkbox to
enable Cloud CDN.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.340
And just a note here--
that Cloud CDN can

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:17.730
use a VM as a back end
service but also a bucket,

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.890
so you could also
do CDN on a bucket.

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.440
And that traffic is going--

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.410
that cachable content is
going to be cached also

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:26.850
at the POP layer--

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.785
so right at the edge.

00:15:29.785 --> 00:15:31.410
Also another
configuration that we have

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.170
there is the health
check, which is

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:35.880
what is the mechanism
that is going

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.390
to be used to check the
liveliness of that instance.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.520
In this case, I'm
using, here, HTTPS.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.300
But you can also configure
using HTTP, using HTTP/2,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.240
configure the timers and do
some other customizations

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.790
like setting, for example,
what type of response

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:52.450
you would expect from
their application

00:15:52.450 --> 00:15:56.320
to consider that healthy.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:16:00.050
Now we have also, as part of
the back end, session affinity.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:04.060
So I have cookie
here, but it can also

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:05.770
be configured as client IP.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:09.760
So client IP would mean that all
the sessions from a certain IP

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:13.840
will be sent to that back
end to maintain the affinity.

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:18.220
Cookie, on the other hand, will
allow the HTTP load balancer

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:20.360
to send a cookie
back to the client.

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:23.740
So if you have multiple
sessions from the same client,

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.240
it would allow a
better distribution

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:28.060
across our back ends.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.470
I have also, here, the
connection training timeout,

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:33.430
which is the timer
that you configure--

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:35.320
in this case, the default--

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:40.640
for the sessions to persist or
just to live in that instance

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.880
when an instance is
removed from the back end.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:45.160
So we want to configure
that as well to make sure

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.900
that, when you remove the
session from the group--

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.110
sorry, when you remove an
instance from the group--

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:53.300
those sessions are not
killed automatically.

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:56.170
And last but not least here,
I'm using also a new feature

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:58.370
I have configured-- which
is available in beta--

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:00.410
which is user defined headers.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.930
So let me magnify here.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.710
So I have configured these
custom request headers

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:09.910
that can be added on the HTTP
layer, on the proxy layer,

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:11.770
and sent back to your back end.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:17.150
In this case, I'm adding here
those options, client region,

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.233
city, rtt.

00:17:19.233 --> 00:17:21.400
So the back ends can take
a look at that information

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:25.099
on a per-session basis and
make smarter decisions,

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:27.940
but it's better if
I show you how that

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:30.230
looks like in the web page.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:36.130
So I have configured-- this is
the landing page for that VIP,

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:38.720
and here we can see some of the
information that I mentioned.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:40.640
So the first one
is the client IP,

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:44.290
which is the public IP that
my client is using-- probably

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:46.310
a netted IP in this case.

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:48.940
The second one is the load
balancer IP, which is the VIP.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.790
That's the same one that we
saw in the configuration.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.680
The third one is
the proxy IP, which

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:54.980
is for the second session--

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.580
what's going to be the source
IP of that second session.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:58.780
The server information.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:00.280
And here we can see
the user defined

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.410
headers that are inserted.

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:05.150
So my connection is
coming from San Francisco,

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:07.360
so I would be a little
bit alarmed if it was

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:09.820
coming from a different place.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:12.550
Here's my RTT-- in this
case, 3 milliseconds.

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:15.670
The lat, long, the TLS
version, and ciphers.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:18.730
So this is custom.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:21.610
I mean you don't need to
add all the variables,

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:24.290
but if you want to add some
of them, that's how you do it.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:35.510
Now going back here, I
just want to show, also,

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:37.910
the second back end
that I have configured.

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:40.640
We are going to use it for
the network security demo,

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:42.770
but the only thing that
I want to highlight

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:45.290
is I'm using a
different protocol here.

00:18:45.290 --> 00:18:47.960
So for this back
end, I'm using HTTP/2

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:51.020
instead of HTTPS, which is
also another feature that

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:52.260
is released in beta.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.260
So for the communication
between the proxy layer

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.790
and the back end,
you can actually

00:18:55.790 --> 00:19:01.280
leverage HTTP/2 for better
latency of your application.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.990
Let's take a look at
the instance group--

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:05.830
at one of those.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:12.210
Actually before we do that,
just one thing that I wanted

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:14.550
to explain to make
sure it's clear

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:16.810
is when you configure
those back ends,

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:18.060
you add those instance groups.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:21.765
That could be managed or
unmanaged, as Babi explained.

00:19:21.765 --> 00:19:23.640
On those instance groups,
you have the option

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:27.510
to set the capacity-- so what we
can see here on the right side.

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:43.100
So if we go back there.

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:46.560
So what we can see here is the
max RPS that I have configured.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.290
In this case, the way it works
is [? I will ?] configure,

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:52.890
on the managed instance group
basis, the amount of requests

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:54.270
that each VM can handle--

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.720
in this case, 100 RPS,
Requests Per Second.

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:00.368
And then the max
capacity that that VM

00:20:00.368 --> 00:20:01.410
can handle in this case--

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:02.490
100%.

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.650
Now if I combine
those two, it means

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:09.810
each VM can handle 100
RPS, and if I set that

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:12.840
with the auto scaling,
it means that 80%

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:16.770
of that capacity, 80 RPS,
auto scale up and start

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.630
a new instance.

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.520
Also note, that in
the second instance

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:23.370
group that I have defined
here, auto scaling is off,

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.740
and I have set that
to a capacity of 10%.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.293
So this is a great use case
to use for canary deployments.

00:20:29.293 --> 00:20:30.960
So if you have a new
web server that you

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:34.260
want to deploy and just
send just a small fraction

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:37.860
of your traffic, you can do that
and have a new instance group

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:39.600
just using part of--

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:42.060
just handling part
of that traffic.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.530
And you're going to
see that in action.

00:20:44.530 --> 00:20:48.270
But just before we do that,
I just wanted to show you,

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.910
very quickly, the instance
group configuration.

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:54.960
So the first part is the
named port that I mentioned.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:58.120
In this case, I'm using
443 encrypted traffic,

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:01.050
but you could also use a
different port if you wanted.

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:03.180
Then the next one is
the instance template,

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:06.630
which what is the instance
that is being used here

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:10.680
to create that VM, so you
can customize that and load

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:13.920
with your own software
and the required software

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:14.910
that you need.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:16.890
Auto scaling is on,
as we mentioned.

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:19.860
And I just wanted to highlight
the cooldown period, which

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.680
is the time that you
need to make sure

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:25.660
that your application takes to
be ready to process traffic.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:28.290
So in this case, I
changed to 30 seconds

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:29.760
because it's a
simple web server,

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:32.218
but you may want to customize
according to your application

00:21:32.218 --> 00:21:33.240
requirements.

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:34.770
And the last one
is auto healing,

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:38.712
so you can enable auto
healing on the group

00:21:38.712 --> 00:21:40.170
here because it's
going-- what it's

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:43.710
going to do is health check that
instance based on the criteria

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:44.340
that you set.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.110
And you get also
another timeout.

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.630
Now just note that,
with the auto healing,

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:51.930
if for some reason that
instance is marked as unhealthy,

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.610
it's going to be
destroyed and recreated.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:59.130
So it will take more time
as compared to just pulling

00:21:59.130 --> 00:22:02.190
an instance from the group.

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:06.230
Now I'm also sending
traffic here,

00:22:06.230 --> 00:22:09.970
so let's take just a quick look
at how the monitoring works

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:10.630
in this case.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.860
So I have three different
load generators-- one in Asia,

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.350
another one in North America,
another one in Europe.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:25.230
They are sending
traffic here, and you

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:31.740
can see that for the
European one the traffic--

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:34.050
the target is the
back end that is used

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:35.790
in Europe because it's close.

00:22:35.790 --> 00:22:37.290
And since I'm
sending a little bit

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:39.750
below 200 RPS,
which is the max, I

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:42.960
can see here that it's
being alerted that usage

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.780
is approaching capacity.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:47.940
Now another behavior
that we can see here also

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:51.840
is traffic being spilled
over from the Asian and North

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.640
American load generators, being
sent to my new instance group--

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:58.800
just a small fraction
of that traffic

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:00.420
just like I wanted,
because I just

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:03.960
wanted 10% of the
traffic being sent there.

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:08.500
And also another thing that
I wanted to show you is I

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:09.910
have created--

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:12.340
there's a new dashboard
here, showing also

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:13.760
the protocol distribution.

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:15.460
So this is based on
a new feature that

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:17.350
is available in alpha,
which is the login

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:19.570
and monitoring of the
HTTP load balancer,

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:21.850
so we are going to
talk a little bit more

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:23.920
about what is available.

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.830
But we can see, here, the number
of requests being generated

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:29.800
by my load generators-- the
same ones that you saw--

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:32.170
and how is the
protocol distribution?

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:33.660
So each one of
those is sending--

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.670
is using a different
HTTP version.

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:38.270
Now back to Babi.

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.312
BABI SEAL: Can you go back
to the slides, please?

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:46.090
So some of you have
internal services

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:48.400
that you run on Google Cloud.

00:23:48.400 --> 00:23:51.730
You want to scale and grow
them behind a virtual IP

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:56.260
address that's only accessible
from your internal instances.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:24:00.400
And for these use cases, we
have the internal load balancer.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:02.980
So for the layer 4
internal load balancer,

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:07.270
you're effectively load
balancing behind an RFC 1918

00:24:07.270 --> 00:24:09.340
private virtual IP address.

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:13.802
You are load balancing TCP and
UDP, similar to a network load

00:24:13.802 --> 00:24:17.680
balancer, and doing the
2, 3, or 5-tuple hashing.

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:21.220
Your client IP is preserved,
and you can have TCP, HTTP,

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:23.110
or HTTPS health checks.

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:25.210
Now the key takeaway is this.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:27.730
There is really no middle proxy.

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:31.960
Effectively, there's
really no load balancer.

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:35.560
The way it works is we have
our underlying software defined

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:37.510
networking layer, Andromeda.

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:40.930
That takes care of doing
the connection tracking--

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:42.460
the consistent hashing.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:44.800
And what it does
is it sends traffic

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:48.250
directly from the client
VM to the back end.

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:50.170
That's how the client
IP is preserved.

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:52.180
But what's even more
important to note

00:24:52.180 --> 00:24:54.610
is that this is a very
scale out architecture.

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:56.920
Because there is
no choke point, you

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:01.580
can have very performance
load balancer.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:04.130
If you look, the data model
seems vaguely familiar.

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.850
Yes, it is pretty consistent
with our global data model.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:09.710
Except over here,
the forwarding rule,

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:11.390
which is regional
forwarding rule

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:13.310
as opposed to a global
forwarding rule,

00:25:13.310 --> 00:25:15.140
has an RFC 1918 VIP.

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:17.240
You specify the
protocol TCP/IP--

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:18.770
I mean, TCP or UDP.

00:25:18.770 --> 00:25:21.320
And then you can have
up to five ports,

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:24.680
and it points directly to a
regional back end service.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:27.170
Now one thing we went
and did this year

00:25:27.170 --> 00:25:31.730
is to give you the flexibility
to specify all ports.

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:35.020
Now think of use cases such
as a Pivotal Cloud Foundry

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:36.890
where they have
multiple applications,

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:39.170
each on a separate TCP port.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:41.540
Well, you can load balance
them with really good entropy

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:44.120
behind a single load
balancer, as opposed

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:45.860
to creating, you
know, forwarding

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:48.140
rules for different ports.

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.390
You can have firewall rules
to protect access to ports

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:53.940
that you don't want traffic on.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:56.820
Now a lot of our customers
also have requirements

00:25:56.820 --> 00:26:01.770
for running certain applications
in active standby mode,

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:03.780
and I'm happy to
announce that we just

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:08.070
did a beta for something we
call our failover groups.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:09.960
And what it allows
you to do is you

00:26:09.960 --> 00:26:12.240
can designate certain
instance groups

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:16.070
as primary and certain
instance groups as secondary.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:19.500
When the health of the
instances in the primary group

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:22.560
go below a certain
threshold, the load balancer

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:25.380
will just automatically
failover the traffic

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.320
to the standby instance group.

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:28.470
You know, it's in beta.

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:30.030
We'd love for you to try it out.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:34.940
Give your feedback, and
we can go from there.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:36.990
Now in my initial
slide, I mentioned

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:38.700
load balancers deep dive.

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.140
There was a sixth load
balancer in there.

00:26:41.140 --> 00:26:44.850
We do have a layer 7
internal load balancer.

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:46.680
It is in alpha.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:50.700
It does support both Compute
as well as Kubernetes--

00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:52.380
container-based Kubernetes.

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:57.090
Under the hood, it uses the
Android-based sidecar proxy.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:02.765
That's performant, feature
rich, as well as open source.

00:27:02.765 --> 00:27:04.890
You don't have to worry
about the management of it,

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:06.270
because GCP--

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:10.290
Google Cloud manages the
load balancer for you.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:12.120
It's currently a
regional service,

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:17.520
but making it globally available
is something on our roadmap.

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:19.430
So now I'm going to
hand it over to Oz

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:22.820
to talk about container
native load balancing.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.160
OSVALDO COSTA:
Thanks again, Babi.

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:28.410
So now we're going to talk a
little bit about the network

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:32.420
endpoint groups that
are used, or leveraged,

00:27:32.420 --> 00:27:34.440
by the container
native load balancing.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:37.197
So on the left side here,
the blue one-- what we have

00:27:37.197 --> 00:27:38.780
is the traditional
way to load balance

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:41.180
across Kubernetes
clusters, which

00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:43.320
would be targeting the nodes.

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:46.265
So in that case, the
instance group would have--

00:27:46.265 --> 00:27:48.320
or the load balancer
would have the visibility

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:51.860
of two different nodes only,
and doesn't know about the pods.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.690
So it will load
balance and distribute

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:56.570
the traffic across those
different nodes only.

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:59.710
When the traffic gets to
those nodes, then kube-proxy--

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:01.580
that will be running
on that node.

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:07.010
Then we'll use, if needed, IP
masquerade to the source net

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:10.820
and may send that traffic
to a different node--

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:11.960
to a different pod.

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:14.240
That different node may
be in a different zone,

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:17.460
so you may have inter-zone
communication in that case.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:21.560
So for this behavior, then
you have two different things

00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:22.530
that apply here.

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:24.200
The first one is the
source net, so you

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:26.980
don't have the source
client anymore because it's

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:28.100
a blind source net.

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:31.010
And the second one, you have
a higher network utilization,

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.070
because now you have IP
tables being applied,

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.080
and traffic that
gets to one port--

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.210
to one node-- being
sent to another node--

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:39.760
to a different pod.

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:42.890
Then now we introduced the
network endpoint groups concept

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:45.810
which is also available in beta.

00:28:45.810 --> 00:28:47.840
So the network endpoint
groups concept--

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:51.200
the load balancer will now have
visibility to the pod layer--

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.370
to the pod level--

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:55.680
by using IP and port pairs.

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:58.280
So in this example here,
instead of targeting

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:01.160
two different nodes, it will
target five different pods.

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:03.510
So you have better latency.

00:29:03.510 --> 00:29:05.480
You can expect better latency--

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:08.300
better network utilization
because we don't have traffic

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:10.010
getting to one
node, and then being

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:11.543
translated to a different one--

00:29:11.543 --> 00:29:13.710
and now also better health
checking, because instead

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:16.350
of health checking the node
and then gets translated,

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:19.170
it just health tracked
the pods directly.

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:23.480
And that is also available
by adding that annotation

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:25.080
that I had added
here to the screen,

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:26.540
but let's see this live.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:29.340
So can we go back
to the demo, please?

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:30.270
Thank you.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:34.310
So I have created two different
clusters in this case--

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:37.070
identical clusters--
so each one of them

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:44.450
with three nodes and six pods.

00:29:44.450 --> 00:29:45.440
And six pods.

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:51.350
And then I have exposed
both of them using ingress--

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:55.060
and one of them with the network
endpoint group's annotation,

00:29:55.060 --> 00:29:56.110
as I mentioned.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:29:57.550
And just to make
sure that we know

00:29:57.550 --> 00:30:01.750
how the pods are distributed
here, if we use this command--

00:30:01.750 --> 00:30:03.430
this kubectl
command-- then we can

00:30:03.430 --> 00:30:07.480
see that Kubernetes
allocated those pods

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:09.740
across different nodes a
little bit differently.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:12.970
So if we take a look at the
network endpoint, the neg

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:17.020
cluster, then the
pods got allocated--

00:30:17.020 --> 00:30:19.450
three pods in one node,
then two in the second one,

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.830
and one in the third one.

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:22.690
And the non-negs was a
little bit different,

00:30:22.690 --> 00:30:28.300
because it got four pods
in one node and then one

00:30:28.300 --> 00:30:30.940
in each additional node.

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:33.190
Now I am also
sending traffic here.

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:34.660
I have traffic generators.

00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:37.600
So if we go back to
the load balancer,

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:40.300
let's see how this traffic
is being distributed.

00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:43.660
So first let's take a
look at the non-neg one,

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:45.640
so the first thing is
the instance groups.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:48.820
So it sees three different
instance groups, and that's it.

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.552
And it just has node awareness.

00:30:51.552 --> 00:30:59.610
Now if you go to Monitoring, I'm
sending around 5,500 requests

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:02.110
per second, and since it
does not have visibility

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:04.230
into the number of
nodes, those requests

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:07.425
are just evenly distributed
across the nodes irrespective

00:31:07.425 --> 00:31:09.300
of the number of pods
that are healthy there.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:22.630
Now if we take a look at
the neg one, since the neg--

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:25.567
since the load balancer now
has visibility into the pods,

00:31:25.567 --> 00:31:27.400
then we can see that
the traffic is actually

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:31.100
distributed according to
the number of pods there.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:34.300
So the first node is
getting traffic here

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:37.370
for around 1,200
requests per second.

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:38.950
The second one is
getting three times

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:42.160
that, and the third
one-- two times that.

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:46.300
Now if we take a look also
at our custom dashboard--

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:49.780
that I also built using the same
login and monitoring feature

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:51.040
that is available in alpha--

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:53.200
we can see the number of
requests there reaching--

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:54.910
and also the latency.

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:57.960
And break the latency
based on the first session

00:31:57.960 --> 00:31:59.180
and the second session.

00:31:59.180 --> 00:32:03.040
So we can see here that,
even though the same amount

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:06.100
of resources are dedicated
to both clusters,

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:10.310
the neg-enabled one
is able to handle

00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:14.970
7,000 requests-- so 2,000
more that the non-neg one.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:17.500
And in terms of latency,
what this graph here

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:22.900
means is on the 99th percentile,
or all sessions under--

00:32:22.900 --> 00:32:27.580
99% of the sessions,
for the neg one

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:31.840
it took around 57
milliseconds to magnify--

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:34.430
so 57 milliseconds.

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:37.990
Now the non-neg took
106 milliseconds

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:42.220
in terms of latency, so it took
almost twice the amount of time

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:44.440
to respond to those sessions.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:48.280
And just to make sure
that there are no tricks,

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:51.620
I have also added here
the front end latency,

00:32:51.620 --> 00:32:54.170
so it's not significant.

00:32:54.170 --> 00:32:57.070
So the latency is
really on the back end.

00:32:57.070 --> 00:32:58.735
And also just to
highlight the point

00:32:58.735 --> 00:33:00.610
that I mentioned in
terms of network traffic,

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:03.370
even though it's a very simple
web server that I have running

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:08.740
here, the neg one consumes
half of the amount of traffic

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:11.210
as compared to the non-neg.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:13.390
So if you had those nodes
just like in this case

00:33:13.390 --> 00:33:14.980
across different
zones, you would

00:33:14.980 --> 00:33:19.028
be paying for more traffic
as well to cross those zones.

00:33:19.028 --> 00:33:19.570
Back to Babi.

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:28.710
BABI SEAL: So now
you've built a service,

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:32.330
and it's now very important
that you secure your edge

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:36.030
to protect it against DDoS
attacks, either volumetric

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:38.850
or application layer.

00:33:38.850 --> 00:33:41.250
Before we get
started, every network

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:42.780
security at Google Cloud--

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:46.290
it's a shared responsibility
between you and Google.

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:48.780
We strive our best to
protect our network

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:53.530
as much as possible, but we also
give you tools to protect it.

00:33:53.530 --> 00:33:55.140
We give you tools.

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:56.910
And our best practice
that we recommend

00:33:56.910 --> 00:33:59.180
is take a defense
in depth approach.

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:01.350
Secure it at all layers.

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:06.360
So please secure it within
your VPC, in your VPC,

00:34:06.360 --> 00:34:10.530
access to your VPC, access to
Google services, from your VPC.

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:11.790
And secure your edge.

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.460
We're going to talk about
securing your edge soon,

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.100
but a lot of these
individual topics

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:20.695
have very good coverage
in sessions here at Next.

00:34:20.695 --> 00:34:23.070
And I would strongly recommend
that you go take them out,

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.659
and do your due
diligence in terms

00:34:24.659 --> 00:34:26.940
of figuring out
the best security

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:31.179
solution for your requirements.

00:34:31.179 --> 00:34:36.280
Now at Google, we strongly
recommend, as a best practice,

00:34:36.280 --> 00:34:40.480
that you run TLS
everywhere that you can.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:43.940
This is for your data privacy
and your data integrity.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:47.139
So we don't charge
extra for encrypted

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:49.179
versus unencrypted traffic.

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:53.980
We give you HTTPS
and SSL proxies.

00:34:53.980 --> 00:34:55.389
We'll talk about
our manage certs

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:58.520
that help you apply certs
to talk to your clients.

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:01.870
We also recommend that
you can apply self sign

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:04.630
certs from your
back end instances

00:35:04.630 --> 00:35:07.570
to your load balancer, so
net net, as this diagram

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:12.130
illustrates, have HTTP
and TLS running everywhere

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:17.420
and have better privacy
and data integrity.

00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:19.010
If you want to run
multiple domains

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:22.370
behind a single virtual IP
address and port of the load

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:25.530
balancer, we support that.

00:35:25.530 --> 00:35:27.870
Managed certs-- we
will help you reduce

00:35:27.870 --> 00:35:30.420
your toil of procuring
the cert and managing

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:32.010
the lifecycle of the cert.

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:34.800
All you have to do, as
this slide illustrates,

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:38.310
is just specify the domain
that you want to secure,

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:42.033
and Google will procure the
certificate in very short order

00:35:42.033 --> 00:35:43.200
and keep you up and running.

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:50.750
You can use SSL policies to
specify the minimum TLS version

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:52.880
and the SSL features
that you want

00:35:52.880 --> 00:35:57.530
to enable on your HTTPS load
balancer and your SSL proxies.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:36:02.000
You can use Google
preconfigured profile

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:05.090
for some of these
features, or you can

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:08.030
choose to have your own custom.

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:10.520
Let's say you have a
strict requirement where

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:13.190
you want to be very
explicit in the ciphers,

00:36:13.190 --> 00:36:16.220
and the TLS version that you
want to deploy in your network.

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:20.480
You can use custom
SSL policies for that.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:22.300
Now securing your edge.

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:24.550
And again, take a
layered approach.

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:26.470
Use a global load balancer.

00:36:26.470 --> 00:36:28.990
Use it in concert
with Cloud Armor.

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:31.840
Use it in concert with
Identity-aware Proxy,

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:34.670
and layer it with
firewall rules.

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:37.355
So with the Google global
network and our global load

00:36:37.355 --> 00:36:41.080
balancer, we are able to
absorb, mitigate, and, you know,

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:43.570
dissipate a lot of the
volumetric layer 3 and layer

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.040
4 attacks.

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:48.070
So that, you get with
the global load balancer.

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:50.200
Now for application
layer attacks,

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:54.122
we recommend that you take a
look at a Cloud Armor solution.

00:36:54.122 --> 00:36:56.330
There's actually a lot of
really good talks over here

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:57.760
at Next with Cloud Armor.

00:36:57.760 --> 00:36:59.410
But essentially
with Cloud Armor,

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:04.090
you can specify security
policies such as IP allow/deny

00:37:04.090 --> 00:37:06.310
lists and geo-based
access control,

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:08.260
in addition to
protecting protection

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:13.540
against cross site scripting
or SQL injection attacks.

00:37:13.540 --> 00:37:16.060
Now layer that with
Identity-aware Proxy.

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:18.730
Now this is the whole beyond
[? carb ?] story, so based

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:22.240
on the identity and
context of the user,

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:26.050
you can authenticate the user
and authorize their access

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.680
to back end services.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:29.800
So, layering security
levels on top.

00:37:29.800 --> 00:37:32.180
And all of this work in
concert with each other.

00:37:32.180 --> 00:37:34.750
So if your Identity-aware
proxy says no,

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:38.650
and your Cloud Armor says
yes, it will default to no.

00:37:38.650 --> 00:37:42.550
In addition, because our load
balancer proxies come from very

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:45.405
well-defined IP addresses,
why don't you block--

00:37:45.405 --> 00:37:46.780
you know, your
firewall should be

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:50.620
configured to only allow those
traffic from the load balancer.

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:53.200
Don't open it up
to the internet.

00:37:53.200 --> 00:37:55.390
So take a defense
in depth approach,

00:37:55.390 --> 00:37:57.940
apply multiple solutions
that we provide for you,

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:01.790
and protect your edge.

00:38:01.790 --> 00:38:03.800
I'm going to give it
to Oz to kind of show

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:05.360
all of this in action.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:08.170
OSVALDO COSTA: Thanks, Babi.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:11.020
So here I'm going to show a
little bit of the security

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:12.880
features that I have configured.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:16.020
Some of them I actually
showed already.

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:20.140
So the first one is
the managed certs--

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:23.030
the Google managed certs
that I have enabled here.

00:38:23.030 --> 00:38:27.100
So as we can see, it's active,
and it's a super simple feature

00:38:27.100 --> 00:38:28.600
to deploy.

00:38:28.600 --> 00:38:31.300
The way to configure
it is you have

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:32.830
to enable when you're in DNS.

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:35.725
And then once you do that,
make sure that you add the A

00:38:35.725 --> 00:38:37.180
record-- the quad-A record--

00:38:37.180 --> 00:38:40.190
pointing to your VIPs, and
those VIPs are configured

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:41.790
pointing to your forwarding--

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:43.690
to our back ends
and are healthy.

00:38:43.690 --> 00:38:46.790
Once you do that,
then the certs should

00:38:46.790 --> 00:38:50.450
to take a few minutes just
to be alive and ready.

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:55.600
I have also attached, here, an
SSL policy restricting the TLS

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:58.420
version, so let's take a look
how that is actually working.

00:39:05.020 --> 00:39:07.020
So I have my default
policy, and I

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:11.010
have defined a new one which
restricts the minimum DNS

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:15.810
version to 1.2 and the set
of ciphers to a restricted.

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:21.880
So if we actually Edit here, we
could even select a custom list

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:23.930
and select only the
ciphers that I want.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:28.090
So I am exposing
that here on my VIP,

00:39:28.090 --> 00:39:32.240
and this is attached
to the front end.

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:35.870
Now if we go here
to my cloudshell,

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:39.590
and I try to connect
using TLS 1.2,

00:39:39.590 --> 00:39:42.820
we can see that HTTP/2
responded with a 200.

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:43.760
It was fine.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:47.570
Now if I change
to 1.1, then I get

00:39:47.570 --> 00:39:50.540
an error of not being accepted.

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:52.730
And the last part that I
wanted to show you here

00:39:52.730 --> 00:39:53.930
is Cloud Armor.

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:55.940
In this case, I'm using
Cloud Armor with the

00:39:55.940 --> 00:39:58.550
allow and deny lists, so
basically what I'm doing

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:02.840
is setting the
default behavior--

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:05.300
so setting the default
behavior to deny,

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:08.477
and I'm customizing the
response-- in this case, a 403.

00:40:08.477 --> 00:40:10.310
But it could be, also,
a different response.

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:13.110
It could be a 502, for example.

00:40:13.110 --> 00:40:15.680
And then I'm setting
an allow list--

00:40:15.680 --> 00:40:18.770
in this case, setting here the
ranges from the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:40:18.770 --> 00:40:23.420
so everyone connected can
actually reach this VIP.

00:40:23.420 --> 00:40:27.980
And then I'm targeting
that second back end

00:40:27.980 --> 00:40:29.810
that I have configured,
so everything

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:32.300
that goes through to
slash secure slash

00:40:32.300 --> 00:40:33.530
is where I'm attaching it.

00:40:36.200 --> 00:40:39.580
So if I go to my landing
page and just type secure,

00:40:39.580 --> 00:40:42.750
you can see it's a
different landing page.

00:40:42.750 --> 00:40:50.240
And now if I try from
cloudshell, I get a 403,

00:40:50.240 --> 00:40:53.640
so it's being blocked.

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:54.140
Babi?

00:40:58.560 --> 00:41:01.070
BABI SEAL: So now
you've secured your edge

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:03.123
and deployed your services.

00:41:03.123 --> 00:41:04.790
The next thought that
comes to your mind

00:41:04.790 --> 00:41:07.070
is how do I optimize
my services?

00:41:07.070 --> 00:41:09.290
And you'd like to
optimize them for latency,

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:11.960
and then optimize them for cost.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:15.020
We've already talked about our
distributed edge infrastructure

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:17.750
and the latency benefits
that you get out of that.

00:41:17.750 --> 00:41:19.850
A few additional
takeaways-- when

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:23.240
you do SSL termination at the
edge, you get better latency.

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:26.540
When you have PCP
retransmissions from the edge,

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.630
you get much better
latency performance.

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:33.440
Talking about TCP optimizations,
after the first connection

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:37.100
is established from the Google
front end to the server,

00:41:37.100 --> 00:41:39.920
we can then optimize for
subsequent connections

00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:41.150
to round trip latencies.

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:44.090
We can eliminate most of
those round trip times

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:47.760
and optimize for a
handshake latency.

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:51.080
Now a really nice way
to optimize for latency,

00:41:51.080 --> 00:41:53.780
especially if you have
content that's cachable,

00:41:53.780 --> 00:41:55.280
is cache it at the edge.

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:57.320
Use Cloud CDN, and
all you have to do

00:41:57.320 --> 00:42:00.050
is enable a checkbox on
your back end service.

00:42:00.050 --> 00:42:02.860
And you'll be serving content
and delighting your users

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:05.725
with their latency experience.

00:42:05.725 --> 00:42:07.100
In fact, there
was a public study

00:42:07.100 --> 00:42:10.260
done by cedexis.com that
compared Google Cloud

00:42:10.260 --> 00:42:12.630
CDN with other providers.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:14.450
You can go-- refer that link.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:16.370
And it showed that
Google Cloud CDN

00:42:16.370 --> 00:42:19.580
had lower latency, better
throughput, and much better

00:42:19.580 --> 00:42:22.710
availability.

00:42:22.710 --> 00:42:25.410
How do you make the web faster?

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:28.590
Now this is something we at
Google spend a lot of time

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:32.550
working on and thinking
about, and if we have any--

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:34.650
my two takeaways
from this session

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:37.320
is please go take
a look at QUIC.

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.270
Take a look at HTTP/2.

00:42:39.270 --> 00:42:43.250
And see if it can help
provide you latency benefits.

00:42:43.250 --> 00:42:47.780
So QUIC stands for Quick
UDP Internet Connections.

00:42:47.780 --> 00:42:51.530
If you've used Chrome,
or you've used YouTube,

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.240
your traffic has
gone on top of QUIC.

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:58.070
QUIC is UDP-based
transport that's encrypted,

00:42:58.070 --> 00:43:01.220
and it's optimized for HTTPS.

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:05.060
Among its many features, the
three that help improve latency

00:43:05.060 --> 00:43:07.260
are quicker connections
setup times,

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:09.620
multiplexing of
different streams,

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:13.830
and no head-of-line blocking.

00:43:13.830 --> 00:43:17.880
So when cedexis was doing the
study of CDN throughput, when

00:43:17.880 --> 00:43:20.160
we went and turned on
QUIC, the throughput

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.320
increased significantly.

00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:24.583
That was because
with lower latency

00:43:24.583 --> 00:43:26.250
we were able to set
up more connections,

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:27.840
and with more
connections we were

00:43:27.840 --> 00:43:30.700
able to get a higher throughput.

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:33.510
Now the diagram on the
right shows the advantages

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:36.510
that QUIC has in terms of
quicker connection setup times.

00:43:36.510 --> 00:43:39.810
When you think of HTTP
running over TCP and TLS,

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:43.650
consider all the handshakes
that are involved with that

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:45.630
HTTP over TCP and TLS.

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:49.140
And think, how paired with
QUIC, it's a lot quicker.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.900
It's even more quicker if the
server has been seen before,

00:43:51.900 --> 00:43:53.430
when the latency is just 0.

00:43:56.070 --> 00:44:00.840
We also support HTTP/2 not just
from the client to the load

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:05.190
balancer, but also from the
load balancer to the back end.

00:44:05.190 --> 00:44:09.300
Doing this enables
us to support gRPC,

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:13.560
and what it enables us to do
is support a stream-based load

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:16.425
balancing of gRPC streams
to the back end service.

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:21.530
Now optimizing for cost.

00:44:21.530 --> 00:44:24.300
You know, when you
egress the data center,

00:44:24.300 --> 00:44:27.510
you incur egress
bandwidth charges.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:30.100
But if you're optimizing
for performance,

00:44:30.100 --> 00:44:32.760
we recommend that
your return traffic

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:37.650
do take the Google's performant
and well-provisioned network

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:40.410
and exit it at the
closest point of presence

00:44:40.410 --> 00:44:44.020
to the user to give the
best user experience.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:46.650
But let's say you have
certain workloads that

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:48.750
are cost sensitive.

00:44:48.750 --> 00:44:50.940
They are not mission
critical, and you

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:53.100
want to optimize for cost.

00:44:53.100 --> 00:44:56.165
Well, we then give you the
option of the standard tier,

00:44:56.165 --> 00:44:57.540
and with the
standard tier, it'll

00:44:57.540 --> 00:45:01.920
take standard ISP transit to
egress the data center just

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:03.630
like other Cloud providers.

00:45:03.630 --> 00:45:07.380
And you'll save in cost savings.

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:09.090
You can turn it on
per load balancer,

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:10.560
or you can turn
it on per project.

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:15.560
And user-defined headers.

00:45:15.560 --> 00:45:18.300
So Oz had shown a really
cool demo of this.

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:19.580
So what you can do is--

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:21.530
at the load balancer
level, you can

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:27.320
configure custom request headers
to be added to those requests.

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:32.420
You can configure for collecting
the geolocation, the TLS

00:45:32.420 --> 00:45:36.020
parameters, the smooth round
trip latency between the load

00:45:36.020 --> 00:45:37.720
balancer and the client.

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:40.160
Capture all that information
from the load balancer.

00:45:40.160 --> 00:45:42.020
Send it to your
back end instance,

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:44.180
then the application
logic in the back end

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:46.100
can use that
information and then

00:45:46.100 --> 00:45:48.260
use it for the
optimization purposes

00:45:48.260 --> 00:45:49.340
that you are looking for.

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:54.600
So now I'm going to
request Oz to pull it

00:45:54.600 --> 00:45:59.196
all together for this session.

00:45:59.196 --> 00:46:00.660
OSVALDO COSTA: Thanks, Babi.

00:46:00.660 --> 00:46:03.920
So just to summarize a little
bit of the best practices

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.980
that we have, the first point
is how to pick the right load

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:08.360
balancer for you?

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:11.498
We have this chart which is
available in our public site,

00:46:11.498 --> 00:46:13.790
but some of the main points
that I would highlight here

00:46:13.790 --> 00:46:16.850
is make sure you understand
what type of traffic you want--

00:46:16.850 --> 00:46:18.350
external versus internal?

00:46:18.350 --> 00:46:21.123
What type of client,
v6 versus v4?

00:46:21.123 --> 00:46:22.040
What type of protocol?

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.070
Do you have HTTP or HTTPS?

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:25.970
Do you need host and path rules?

00:46:25.970 --> 00:46:26.950
Is it TCP?

00:46:26.950 --> 00:46:28.170
Is it UDP?

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:30.120
How the sessions are
being distributed--

00:46:30.120 --> 00:46:31.700
do you need TLS termination?

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:37.160
So this helps you to pick the
best load balancer for your use

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:38.580
case.

00:46:38.580 --> 00:46:42.590
Now once you have selected
that, a few points that I

00:46:42.590 --> 00:46:43.820
wanted to highlight--

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:45.770
the first one is secure
your service, right.

00:46:45.770 --> 00:46:48.260
So we have seen several
of the best practices

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:51.020
here in the demo, and
also we talked through it.

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:54.480
So TLS everywhere,
that's the first point.

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:57.470
So do TLS on your front
end using your cert--

00:46:57.470 --> 00:46:59.370
or leveraging the
Google managed cert,

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:02.990
so you don't have to be
concerned about renewals.

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:06.110
Use self signed
certs, for example,

00:47:06.110 --> 00:47:08.030
on your instances for
that communication

00:47:08.030 --> 00:47:11.090
between the proxy and the VIP.

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:12.590
Leverage the VPC firewall.

00:47:12.590 --> 00:47:15.980
So as Babi mentioned, make sure
you harden your VPC firewall,

00:47:15.980 --> 00:47:17.870
and it does not
allow-- and do not

00:47:17.870 --> 00:47:20.720
allow traffic that you are
not supposed to expect.

00:47:20.720 --> 00:47:23.060
Use the SSL policies, as well--

00:47:23.060 --> 00:47:25.400
very important for compliance.

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:29.150
So optimized for latency, so
pick the right regions closer

00:47:29.150 --> 00:47:30.530
to your clients.

00:47:30.530 --> 00:47:35.420
Leverage the cross region
global load balancer,

00:47:35.420 --> 00:47:38.240
so make use of the
waterfall algorithm--

00:47:38.240 --> 00:47:40.340
that it's going to
send the traffic

00:47:40.340 --> 00:47:43.670
to the closest available
region based on the RTT.

00:47:43.670 --> 00:47:45.560
If you're using
Kubernetes, take a look

00:47:45.560 --> 00:47:49.180
at the container and native load
balancing for the network end

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:53.360
point groups, because that may
also help you with latency.

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:55.310
Also, optimize for auto scaling.

00:47:55.310 --> 00:47:58.520
Our load balancers, they don't
need to be warmed up, right.

00:47:58.520 --> 00:48:00.090
They are ready to
process traffic.

00:48:00.090 --> 00:48:01.490
However, your back
end instances,

00:48:01.490 --> 00:48:02.540
they need to be ready.

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:05.540
So make sure that you
configure those instances

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:08.780
with the right auto scaling
criteria that you need.

00:48:08.780 --> 00:48:12.180
The timers that you need for
that instance should be ready.

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:14.990
And be careful also
with health checks.

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:18.050
They can be chatty, and the
way that the architecture works

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:20.030
is that there is a
back end manager that

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:22.280
is responsible for doing
all those health checks.

00:48:22.280 --> 00:48:26.000
So you can set those timers
and customize those timers

00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:28.290
based on your
application requirements.

00:48:28.290 --> 00:48:30.290
And another thing
is auto healing.

00:48:30.290 --> 00:48:33.770
It is an option
that you can use.

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:36.710
Just be aware that if
auto healing detects

00:48:36.710 --> 00:48:38.430
that your application
is not ready,

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:41.090
it's going to destroy
that VM and recreate,

00:48:41.090 --> 00:48:43.460
so it will take a longer time.

00:48:43.460 --> 00:48:45.560
If you are using the
global load balancers--

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:48.020
HTTP load balancers,
TCP, or SSL proxy.

00:48:48.020 --> 00:48:50.750
So combine that
with Cloud Armor--

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:54.260
being allow and deny
lists or at the WAF layer

00:48:54.260 --> 00:48:57.260
for all the other features
that Babi mentioned.

00:48:57.260 --> 00:48:58.640
Also use the capacity scaler.

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:00.170
So we talked about it, right?

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:03.320
So the capacity, you can
set to a percentage--

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:04.230
a small percentage.

00:49:04.230 --> 00:49:06.980
So when you have
a new version, you

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:11.230
can then deploy with
just that instance group,

00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:12.980
and just send just a
small percentage just

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.220
to test traffic.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:18.050
And by the way, it doesn't
need to be in the same VPC,

00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:21.073
so it can also be
on a separate VPC.

00:49:21.073 --> 00:49:22.740
Set the appropriate
connection timeouts,

00:49:22.740 --> 00:49:25.290
so make sure you understand
your application--

00:49:25.290 --> 00:49:26.310
what do you expect?

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:28.070
How long those flows--

00:49:28.070 --> 00:49:30.380
they need to be to
be up and running?

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:33.920
And use cookies or
IP-based, as we talked.

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:36.200
Now for the Layer 4 load
balancer and network load

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:39.320
balancer, the default
is the 5-tuple,

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:43.080
so you can get a
better distribution.

00:49:43.080 --> 00:49:46.760
However in some cases, you
may have fragmented packets,

00:49:46.760 --> 00:49:50.320
or even sessions that you
need to do stickiness based

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:51.900
on the source and client IP.

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:53.480
So you can customize that.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:55.010
Also on the ILB--

00:49:55.010 --> 00:49:58.230
Babi mentioned that the ILB
actually does not exist.

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:01.850
So the way it works in the
architecture under the hood

00:50:01.850 --> 00:50:05.000
is that those instances, they
are configured to respond

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:07.520
to packets sent to the VIP.

00:50:07.520 --> 00:50:11.150
So if you're using your custom
VM, that's a common thing.

00:50:11.150 --> 00:50:13.580
Make sure that you add
that to your VM as well,

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:16.945
and allow that VM to respond
to packets sent to the VIP.

00:50:16.945 --> 00:50:18.320
BABI SEAL: I think
we're actually

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.487
at the close of our session.

00:50:19.487 --> 00:50:21.170
But before we go,
please take a moment

00:50:21.170 --> 00:50:23.600
to say a big thanks to
the production crew.

00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:24.680
They were awesome.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:28.370
They work really hard, and I
totally appreciate their work.

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:29.270
